    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The Bertalanffy model (after C.
                Von Bertalanffy) balances synthesis and destruction [3].
                It assume that tumor growth is proportional to surface area,
                since nutrient enters through the surface, and that death is
                proportional the tumor size.
                Thus we arrive at the model given below, also known as the
                surface rule model:
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt}= aV^{2/3}- bV
                </md>
                This model has been successfully applied to describe human tumor
                growth.
                The exact solution of the Von Bertalanffy differential equation
                model is given below.
                <md>
                    V(t) = \left[\frac{a}{b}+\left(V_{0}^{1/3}- \frac{a}{b}\right)e^{-bt/3}\right]^{3}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the maximum tumor size.
                    Do not assume specific values for <m>a</m> or <m>b</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which grows faster as <m>V</m> gets bigger, <m>V^{2/3}</m>
                    or <m>V</m>?
                    (Hint: plot them both on the same graph.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given your answer above, if the tumor gets to a big enough
                    volume, is the tumor going to start shrinking, or will the
                    tumor always continue to grow no matter what?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
