                    and <m>b</m>.
                    (Hint: there are technically two).
                    Determine their stability (stable or unstable?).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The logistic model of population growth was first published by
                Pierre Verhulst in 1845 [11].
                The model assumes a linear decrease of the relative growth rate
                with population size.
                The maximum size is limited by a carrying capacity
                <m>\frac{a}{b}</m>.
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt}= aV\left(1-\frac{V}{a/b}\right) = aV\left(1-\frac{b}{a}V\right).
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the differential equation for tumor volume
                    <m>V(t)</m>.
                    Do not assume specific values for <m>a</m> or <m>b</m>.
                    (You can solve this using technology, or previous
                    knowledge.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the maximum tumor size.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The Gompertz model was created by Benjamin Gompertz in 1825 to
                explain human mortality curves and determine the value of life
                insurances [5].
                It was applied to organism growth a hundred years later.
                The Gompertz model exhibits an exponential decay of the growth
                rate.
                It has been successfully used to model breast and lung cancer
                growth.
                If we let <m>r(t)</m> be the growth rate that exhibits
                exponential decay, then we have a system of differential
                equations given by:
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt}= r(t)V(t), \quad \quad \frac{dr}{dt}= -br(t).
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                However, from the above equations we can rearrange and find
                that:
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dt}\left(\ln V\right) = \frac{1}{V}\frac{dV}{dt}= r(t) = -\frac{1}{b}\frac{dr}{dt}.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Thus,
                <md>
                    \ln(V) = \frac{-r(t)+a}{b}
                </md>
                for some constant <m>a</m>.
                Solving for <m>r(t)</m> leads to <m>r(t) = a-b\ln V</m>.
                Substituting this into the system of two differential equations
                above, we obtain an alternative and more popular form of the
                Gompertz model:
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt}= V(a-b\ln V).
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the differential equation for tumor volume
                    <m>V(t)</m>.
                    Do not assume specific values for <m>a</m> or <m>b</m>.
                    (You can solve this using technology, or previous
                    knowledge.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the maximum tumor size.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the tumor volume was 4 cubic units at some time, and the
                    tumor was shrinking at that time, what would we be able to
                    say about the parameters <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        In the case of multicellular tumor spheroids, cancer growth follows a
        sigmoid curve (S-shaped curve) with three phases: initial exponential
        growth, linear growth, and plateau.
        The Bertalanffy, logistic, and Gompertz models reflect the sigmoid
        nature of growth, while the exponential growth and power law models do
        not.
        We will be using Bertalanffy, logistic, and Gompertz models going
        forward.
        The three models are summarized in the figure below, originating from
        the generalized two-parameter model of growth for biological systems
        [7].
    </p>

    <image source="images/lab2_modelSummary.png" width="75%">
        <shortdescription>
            Nesting scheme for empirical models of growth.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>

    <p>
        <term>Part 2: Model Fitting and Analysis</term>
    </p>

    <p>
        In the second part of this project, you will fit models to real cancer
        data to predict the best fit model for tumor growth, Gompertz, Logistic,
        or Bertalanffy.
        The fitting is performed in python by minimizing the residuals, that is
        the difference between the real data value and what the model predicts
        the data value should be.
    </p>

    <p>
        To measure the <q>goodness or fit</q>, we will want to use the
        normalized mean square error (NMSE):
        <md>
            NMSE = \frac{\sum\limits_{i} (y_{i} - \hat y_{i})^{2}}{\sum\limits_{i} y_{i}^{2}}.
        </md>
        The values <m>y_{i}</m> represent the real data values, the values
        <m>\hat y_{i}</m> represent what your model thinks (predicts) the data
        values should be.
        The NMSE is independent of measurement units and scale.
        A value of NMSE close to zero indicates a good fit of the model.
        You will need to download and clean the data carefully, before you can
        use it in your analysis.
    </p>

    <p>
        <em>
    Tumor Data 1 - Mouse Skin Carcinogenesis
    </em>
    </p>

    <p>
        Carcinogenesis means the formation of cancer.
        The data file named "TransgenicMouseData.xlsx" provided is taken from
        mice tumors that arise in situ (in the original place) from normal skin
        as a result of a chemical mutation, and the tumor develops naturally in
        vivo (in the live mouse).
        The complex dynamics of the formation of the tumor closely resemble that
        found in [6].
    </p>

    <p>
        The excel file consists of several tabs and several experiments
        involving different treatments.
        We want to look at the control data.
        The tab "Main experiment summary" shows the summary of the experiments.
        In particular, the mouse named <q>CM.37</q> has DMSO under the drug
        column, which denotes that the drug delivery device was administrated
        but there was no drug contained in the device.
        Therefore <q>CM.37</q> is a control, and what we want to look at (normal
        tumor growth).
        Under the Experiment column, our mouse friend had the label <q>CM3</q>,
        so go to the tab named <q>Tumor sizes_CM3</q> and locate mouse
        <q>CM.37</q>.
        There were three tumors measured on our mouse friend, we will focus on
        tumor number 1.
        Measurement began when the tumor reached about 1-2 mm in diameter, and
        at each time point the length and width in millimeters (horizontal and
        vertical directions, <m>H^{*}</m> and <m>V^{*}</m> ) were measured three
        times for a more accurate value.
    </p>

